{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-93985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-93985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Q$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"] ["text" "None of the matrices are symmetric"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "None of the matrices are symmetric"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-140504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-93993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Q$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"] ["text" "None of the matrices are symmetric"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the matrices are symmetric"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is symmetric. And, since the sum to two symmetric matrices is symmetric, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-96596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e700caaad5fbab46c7f7285b96aa713a77e590a4b9b1ca90cefa2bc666dc306c"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "q-143686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6bcb6d429dc33fd9498a1ceb45d4e3cac51112ffa7a506ff09b305df73735b9"} {"question" "e-8156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/494cd45f9c85aaa8703ed4c5a0cac805b69cd91bbef979a2c3ff1ffac1592763.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/example-8156.json" "file_sha256" "dc638051d56c8e390d0bdb7f7837a5df2fe9e13fc118791f576763d170eeb275"} {"question" "e-8156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/4db4bdfa8aaeac26238404107f195847eaccde9e595a7d1a0678053e2b149d0e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/example-8156.json" "file_sha256" "dc638051d56c8e390d0bdb7f7837a5df2fe9e13fc118791f576763d170eeb275"} {"question" "e-8478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\n- The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/example-8478.json" "file_sha256" "9889f4f921a1faf1ca55b53fe90e5318cd9ac85d9fb2dd5132b11d41ea521b22"} {"question" "e-8478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/f8d153708f7872d0c96414a77c12379d40c6b60a5f08844945fb8a8f8c1809ab.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/example-8478.json" "file_sha256" "9889f4f921a1faf1ca55b53fe90e5318cd9ac85d9fb2dd5132b11d41ea521b22"}] "usage" {"q-93583" [] "q-96046" [] "e-8156" [] "e-8478" []} "basis" 1742 "retractions" [[17592186162512 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186162608 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $AA^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n2. The sum of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric."] [17592186161703 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186161703 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T$.\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T$.\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21}$, we obtain $R \\neq R^T$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""] [17592186161751 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n2. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric."] [17592186161751 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A$, we have\n$(A + A^{T})^{T}|= A^{T} + (A^{T})^{T}; = A^{T} + A; = A + A^{T}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n$AB|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186160705 17592186160706 17592186160707 17592186160708 17592186160709 17592186161281 17592186161282 17592186161283 17592186161284 17592186161285] "immutable_fields" [[17592186160704 :answer-field/correct] [17592186160704 :answer-field/id] [17592186160704 :answer-field/key] [17592186160704 :answer-field/type] [17592186161280 :answer-field/correct] [17592186161280 :answer-field/id] [17592186161280 :answer-field/key] [17592186161280 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
